content.study:
Article 3 authorises Parliament by law to:
(a) form a new State by separation of territory from any State or by uniting two or more States or parts of States or by uniting any territory to a part of any State;
(b) increase the area of any State;
(c) diminish the area of any State;
(d) alter the boundaries of any State;
(e) alter the name of any State.

Proviso (procedure):
- No Bill for these purposes may be introduced in either House except on the recommendation of the President.
- Where the proposal affects the area, boundaries or name of any of the States, the Bill must be referred by the President to the Legislature of that State for expressing its views within such period as may be specified (or further allowed).

Nature of State views: The reference is for views, not for consent. Parliament is not bound by the State Legislature’s opinion; the requirement is procedural (consultation), not a veto.

Article 4: A law under Article 3 may amend the First and Fourth Schedules and contain supplemental, incidental and consequential provisions; such a law is not an amendment of the Constitution for the purposes of Article 368.

Federal character: Article 3 is a strong unitary feature within the federal structure — Parliament can reorganise States without State consent, subject only to the Presidential recommendation and reference procedure.
